WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.570
Value placeholders normally represent data
expressions.

2
00:00:03.650 --> 00:00:06.904
They do not generally stand for SQL
grammar such as a table name,

3
00:00:06.984 --> 00:00:10.299
column name, operator, complete predicate,
or sort direction.

4
00:00:10.379 --> 00:00:14.493
Binding a requested column to a
placeholder does not turn it into an identifier;

5
00:00:14.573 --> 00:00:18.502
depending on the database, it may be
rejected or treated as a constant value.

6
00:00:18.582 --> 00:00:22.450
This limitation matters whenever an API
lets a caller choose how a result is

7
00:00:22.530 --> 00:00:24.802
ordered, grouped, projected, or searched.

8
00:00:24.882 --> 00:00:27.829
The safe pattern is a closed
server-controlled mapping.

9
00:00:27.909 --> 00:00:30.794
A request may offer a choice such as name
or created date.

10
00:00:30.874 --> 00:00:34.988
Application code looks up that choice in a
fixed object whose values are trusted

11
00:00:35.068 --> 00:00:37.892
column fragments, rejects anything outside
the keys,

12
00:00:37.972 --> 00:00:40.244
and interpolates only the mapped fragment.

13
00:00:40.324 --> 00:00:44.315
Sort direction can be selected through a
separate mapping to the two intended

14
00:00:44.395 --> 00:00:44.947
keywords.

15
00:00:45.027 --> 00:00:48.036
Raw request text never occupies the
structural position.

16
00:00:48.116 --> 00:00:52.352
Interpolation is acceptable in this narrow
case because its result came entirely

17
00:00:52.432 --> 00:00:54.704
from application-authored alternatives.

18
00:00:54.784 --> 00:00:58.774
Apply the same reasoning to selectable
report columns, optional operators,

19
00:00:58.854 --> 00:01:02.354
table partitions, aggregation choices, and
reusable predicates.

20
00:01:02.434 --> 00:01:06.118
Prefer redesign when a client appears to
need arbitrary SQL structure.

21
00:01:06.198 --> 00:01:09.697
Sometimes a library provides a dedicated
identifier-quoting API;

22
00:01:09.777 --> 00:01:14.198
use it only according to its documentation
and still constrain which identifiers the

23
00:01:14.278 --> 00:01:15.813
operation is meant to expose.

24
00:01:15.893 --> 00:01:19.699
Generic escaping does not decide whether a
syntactically valid column is

25
00:01:19.779 --> 00:01:22.911
appropriate, sensitive, or available in
the current query.

26
00:01:22.991 --> 00:01:27.166
Keep mappings close to the query or expose
them through a typed operation so their

27
00:01:27.246 --> 00:01:28.781
security purpose is visible.

28
00:01:28.861 --> 00:01:32.790
Do not accept a complete ORDER BY or WHERE
fragment from another layer merely

29
00:01:32.870 --> 00:01:35.142
because that layer validated it as a
string.

30
00:01:35.222 --> 00:01:39.642
Validation of length or characters cannot
express the full semantics of SQL grammar.

31
00:01:39.722 --> 00:01:43.590
The important boundary is not between
good-looking and bad-looking text.

32
00:01:43.670 --> 00:01:48.029
It is between a small set of trusted
structural alternatives and untrusted text that

33
00:01:48.109 --> 00:01:49.460
must never become syntax.
